D = {'ripwire-before-you-build': "Starting a FEATURE (multi-symbol work): plan, interface or size estimate from the codebase's real structure — spike viability, ordered plan, API boundary, effort; --lego=Iface lists an interface's contract and every implementor to copy. ONE symbol → reuse-first. A small feature with an obvious home needs none of this.", 'ripwire-change-check': 'Merge-SAFETY of a diff that already exists — yours before you push, or a PR you review: blast radius, tests to run (--affected), a broken contract (--edit-check=SYM), branch conflicts and landing order (--merge-scout), work stranded on old refs. Code QUALITY → quality-bar. Even a one-line leaf fix runs --edit-check.', 'ripwire-efficient': 'About to open more than ~2 files, or paste whole files, to answer one question: map first, then read only the 2-3 files the map ranks highest. The token-and-accuracy discipline for ANY read — less context is measurably MORE accurate. Fires alongside the moment skills, never instead of them.', 'ripwire-find-bug': "You have a SYMPTOM — crash, exception, wrong output, failing test, error string, stack trace — and don't yet know which code is responsible. Ranks candidate symbols; a trace maps onto indexed frames innermost-first; 'I changed X and it broke' → --situ. One clear hit plus one focused read that explains the symptom is enough — fix it.", 'ripwire-fresh-eyes': "Maintenance risk in code you did NOT write, in a subsystem already identified — 'what's gnarly / where's the rot / safe to touch?', before a refactor: hotspots, dead code, clones, nesting profile, bus factor, co-change coupling. Diagnoses the shape; naming the fix, or judging YOUR new code → quality-bar. A single-lens question is a single call.", 'ripwire-graph-query': "A call-graph question the fixed verbs can't phrase — 'which high-complexity functions can reach X?', 'what in src/ has 10+ callers?'. --graph-query: a small closed expression language over the symbol graph — filter by kind, complexity, fan-in; a file or cluster; bounded callers/callees closure; and/or/not. When --callers/--impact alone can't ask it.", 'ripwire-handoff': "Brief someone ELSE on a subsystem — 'hand this area off': purpose, the 2-3 entry points with signatures and bodies, the design docs that say WHY, and hotspot/bus-factor risk scoped to that area, as a compact pasteable brief instead of a wall of source. Understanding it yourself → orient.", 'ripwire-layers': "Architecture HEALTH and ENFORCEMENT — 'is this a dependency mess / any layering violations / enforce module boundaries in CI?': cycles, godfiles, propagation cost, --arch layering rules with a baseline gate, and what to DO about each bad number. Structure overview without gating → orient. One pass answers it.", 'ripwire-mcp': "Wire ripwire into an agent as an MCP server — `ripwire wrap AGENT` (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Windsurf, Gemini, opencode, aider) — and choose the MCP verb mid-task. Also tool HEALTH: a symbol you expected is missing from the ranked output, results look stale after a rebase, 'is my ripwire setup broken?' — is the answer trustworthy right now.", 'ripwire-navigate': "You can NAME the symbol: who calls it, what it calls, the path from A to B, a bounded neighborhood, its full body, or an exact literal/regex/AST-shape match with its enclosing symbol. 'Safe to change X?' = the transitive blast radius, not 1-hop callers. Three or more symbols that must meet → --connect=A,B,C. Run the one verb that fits, then stop.", 'ripwire-opt-remarks': "Triage clang optimization remarks (-Rpass, -Rpass-missed, -fsave-optimization-record) while editing ripwire's OWN C++: 'loop not vectorized', 'will not be inlined', 'load clobbered', LICM failed — is the remark worth a diff, or is LTO/PGO the real fix? Contributor-only: about compiling this tool, never about running it.", 'ripwire-orient': "Landing COLD in an unfamiliar repo or subsystem, before editing: lay of the land, entry points, 'how does X work / where is Y', an architecture overview or module map, or rebuilding context after a compaction. A ladder from a one-screen map up to communities and diagrams; --skipped when the map looks short. A NAMED symbol → navigate. Stop at the first rung that answers.", 'ripwire-perf-target': 'A MEASURED performance problem: start from a benchmark, flame graph or profiler sample, pin the workload, then locate the hot symbol the profile names and test structural hypotheses (complexity, churn, nesting; memory-bound → --field-affinity layout). Static metrics are change-risk signals, not runtime heat. Inspect only the symbols the profile names.', 'ripwire-quality-bar': "Code QUALITY of what YOU just wrote, right before commit / PR / 'it's done': --quality-delta reports only what got WORSE across 10 kinds (complexity, nesting, duplication, dead code, API surface, error masking…), exits non-zero on new debt, then names the refactor a measured shape calls for. Merge safety → change-check. Even a single-line leaf fix runs the one-shot delta.", 'ripwire-reuse-first': "About to write ONE symbol — a function, class, helper, even a 'quick' one-liner — or add a dependency: reuse before you reinvent. Finds the existing building block, the repo's best exemplar to imitate (by ROLE, not text similarity), the duplicate you'd recreate, a dependency already vendored. A whole feature → before-you-build. One --exemplar or --grep call at most.", 'ripwire-router': "Start HERE when unsure which ripwire skill fits, or asked 'which ripwire skill / how do I use ripwire / where do I start'. A moment→skill map — cold start, understand X, plan a feature, write a symbol, review my diff, debug, refactor, perf, security, handoff — plus the two reflexes that leak most: --exemplar before you write, --quality-delta before done. One hop to the right skill.", 'ripwire-security-scan': 'Security review: (1) vet an untrusted SKILL.md, skills/ dir or .mcp.json BEFORE installing it — the injection/exfiltration/path-traversal scanner (CRITICAL blocks the install) plus a manual checklist of shell stanzas; (2) review code on an untrusted-input path (parsing, deserialization, auth, exec/eval): unsafe-fn lint, taint reach, untested seams. One scan pass is the verdict.', 'ripwire-write-tests': "Write tests for EXISTING code that has none — 'this is untested, add coverage' / 'what's missing a test here?'. Finds what no test reaches: --seams ranks untested cross-module edges, --callers gives the outside contract, --affected proves the new test registers. Judging your own diff → change-check. For one target one --seams or --callers pass suffices."}
import pathlib, re, sys, textwrap
root = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else pathlib.Path("skills")
for name, desc in D.items():
    f = root / name / "SKILL.md"
    t = f.read_text(); parts = t.split("---", 2); fm = parts[1]
    m = re.search(r'^description:[ \t]*(.*?)(?=\n[A-Za-z_-]+:|\Z)', fm, re.S | re.M)
    wrapped = "\n".join("  " + l for l in textwrap.wrap(desc, 100))
    fm2 = fm[:m.start()] + "description: >\n" + wrapped + "\n" + fm[m.end():].lstrip("\n")
    f.write_text("---" + fm2 + "---" + parts[2])
for name, desc in sorted(D.items(), key=lambda kv: -len(kv[1])):
    print(f"{len(desc):>4} {name}")
print("total", sum(len(v) for v in D.values()))

